# What does NOT get uploaded by `npx vercel deploy --prod`.
#
# Harvested from mantle-kr-herald. Two incidents shaped this file; both are in this piece's
# README.md. Read it before editing a single line here.
#
# That command uploads the local directory, NOT a commit -- so without this file, what ships is
# whatever happens to be sitting in the working tree, including gitignored credentials that
# `git status` never shows you. Whether the CLI falls back to `.gitignore` when this file is
# absent is not something a deploy should depend on: state it here instead.
#
# EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN BELOW IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING `/`. This is gitignore syntax: an
# unanchored `translation/` matches a directory of that name AT ANY DEPTH, including one your
# serverless function needs. Pin this with a test; see README.md.
#
# `vercel.json` is uploaded regardless of what this file says -- the platform needs it to build.

# --- credentials. The reason this file exists. Unanchored on purpose: an `.env` at any depth is
#     the same mistake wherever it is.
.env
.env.*
/keys/

# --- installed on the platform from the lockfile; uploading them wastes the whole upload.
#     Unanchored on purpose: a nested `node_modules/` is just as pointless to ship.
node_modules/

# --- build artefacts. The build command regenerates these on the platform; shipping a local copy
#     would let a stale bundle win over the one the platform just built.
/web/dist/
/dist/
/.vercel/

# --- not part of the running deployment ---
/tests/
/web/tests/
/docs/
/.github/
/vitest.config.ts
*.md

# --- local-only pipeline data and scheduler units. ANCHORED: unanchored `deploy/` would also match
#     `src/deploy/`, which the function needs. That is the exact mistake described in the header.
/deploy/
/output/
